# Target matrix — <IP> <label> <role>
#
# Copy to config/targets.conf and replace with your own topology. The addresses
# below are RFC 5737 documentation addresses and will not work anywhere.
#
# This file is the measurement. The tools are just what carries it out.
#
# ==========================================================================
# WHY A MATRIX AND NOT A LIST
# ==========================================================================
#
# Treat every measured path as the sum of the segments it crosses. With p
# probes and t targets you get p x t path measurements over some number of
# unknown segments — a linear system, A x = b. What you can determine is not
# "how many targets did I ping" but the RANK of that system.
#
# This has a practical consequence that is easy to get wrong: adding more
# targets BEHIND the same bottleneck adds rows to A without adding rank. Ten
# servers in one rack, measured from one probe, tell you about one uplink.
#
# In the campaign this repository came from, three probes and eight targets
# produced rank 9 against 11 segments: two quantities stayed indeterminable no
# matter how long the measurement ran, and exactly ONE segment was individually
# determinable. Adding a target that TERMINATES ON A SWITCH — the switch's own
# management address — raised the rank to 10 and made four segments individually
# determinable, including the prime suspect. Adding the remaining switches
# brought it to 12-13 of 14.
#
# So: before you start, ask which segments you want separated, and check
# whether your matrix separates them. A target that shares its entire path with
# a target you already have is worth almost nothing.
#
# ==========================================================================
# ROLES
# ==========================================================================
#
# The role is what turns a set of measurements into a verdict. It says what an
# outage of THIS target rules in and rules out.
#
#   symptom      The host that shows the reported problem. Everything is
#                measured relative to this one.
#
#   same-host    Shares the symptom's physical host, but not its VM. Fails
#                together with the symptom -> the host or its virtual switch.
#                Symptom fails alone -> inside the symptom's own VM.
#
#   other-host   Different physical host, same network. Separates "this host"
#                from "this network".
#
#   fabric-ref   An ordinary endpoint deep in the network, usually the gateway.
#                Loss here means the shared path, not any one device.
#
#   uplink-ref   A target reachable only across the uplink between segments.
#                Separates "my floor" from "everything above it".
#
#   client-path  A workstation on the path real users take. The only role that
#                measures what users actually experience; everything else
#                measures what the servers experience.
#
#   switch-ref   A switch's own management address. READ THE WARNING BELOW.
#
# ==========================================================================
# WARNING: switch-ref carries no verdict, on purpose
# ==========================================================================
#
# A switch answers ICMP from its management CPU, not from its forwarding path.
# Under load that CPU delays or discards replies while traffic passes through
# the device untouched. Scored as an ordinary reference, that produces a
# confident "the network is broken" verdict from a device that was forwarding
# perfectly.
#
# Measured, not assumed: in one hour of monitoring, switch targets produced
# apparent outages of up to 24 seconds while EVERY target BEHIND the same
# switch stayed clean in the same window, and other probes saw the same switch
# answering normally at the same moment. Three probes at 5 packets/s each is 15
# packets/s arriving at a CPU that was not built for it. We were measuring our
# own polling.
#
# The visible tell is scatter: the same probe measured 1.6-5.5 ms to the switch
# and 0.6 ms to ordinary hosts in the same segment.
#
# Therefore switch-ref is excluded from the judgement matrix. When only
# switch-ref targets fail, the correlator reports "OUTAGE OUTSIDE THE JUDGEMENT
# MATRIX" rather than silently calling the network clean. Keep these targets —
# they raise the rank and they are how you see the switch itself — but never
# let one decide anything.
#
# ==========================================================================
# PROVE IDENTITY, DO NOT INFER IT
# ==========================================================================
#
# An address is not a device. During the campaign this came from, one address
# changed hands mid-measurement: the access point that had been at .11 was
# powered off, a different device picked up the lease, and anyone checking the
# AP by its address was measuring a stranger and reading its silence as the
# AP's.
#
# Establish identity from LLDP chassis IDs, TLS certificates or HTTP banners.
# Never from the MAC OUI: the vendor is a hint, not an identity.
#
# ==========================================================================

# <IP>         <label>      <role>

# --- the symptom and its neighbours -------------------------------------
192.0.2.20     ts01         symptom
192.0.2.21     hv01         hypervisor
192.0.2.41     mail01       same-host
192.0.2.42     dc01         same-host
192.0.2.43     vm01         other-host
192.0.2.44     sql01        other-host

# --- shared path --------------------------------------------------------
192.0.2.1      gw           fabric-ref
192.0.2.45     scan01       fabric-ref

# --- across the uplink --------------------------------------------------
192.0.2.33     probe3       uplink-ref

# --- what users actually traverse ---------------------------------------
192.0.2.51     ws01         client-path
192.0.2.52     ws02         client-path

# --- switches: raise the rank, carry no verdict (see warning above) -----
192.0.2.2      sw-floor     switch-ref
192.0.2.3      sw-core-a    switch-ref
192.0.2.4      sw-core-b    switch-ref
